1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,140
The night before he was supposed to
go to prison, a schoolteacher

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:08,080
walked into the University of Virginia
Hospital and told the staff

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,270
two things. His head hurt.

4
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:13,950
And he was afraid he was going
to rape his landlady.

5
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:17,120
That isn't a line from a thriller.

6
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:20,000
It's from a case report in a
neurology journal.

7
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:22,560
The man in it has never been named.

8
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:27,350
He was forty, married, a former
corrections officer who had gone back

9
00:00:27,370 --> 00:00:28,820
to school to become a teacher.

10
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:33,090
Over the year before,
something in him had changed.

11
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:37,360
He started collecting pornography,
more and more of it focused on

12
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:41,090
children. He made sexual advances
toward his stepdaughter.

13
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:45,800
She was a child. She told her mother,
and he was found guilty of child

14
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:46,650
molestation.

15
00:00:48,490 --> 00:00:50,020
Then doctors scanned his brain.

16
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:54,530
At the base of his skull, just above
his eyes, a tumor was pushing

17
00:00:54,550 --> 00:00:57,600
into a region that helps hold
our impulses in check.

18
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:01,070
Surgeons removed it.

19
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:02,730
The urges went away.

20
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:07,190
He finished a treatment program, and
months later he went home.

21
00:01:08,690 --> 00:01:12,000
Then, ten months after the surgery,
the headaches came back.

22
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:13,780
So did the secret collecting.

23
00:01:14,530 --> 00:01:15,640
They scanned him again.

24
00:01:16,370 --> 00:01:17,720
The tumor had grown back.

25
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:20,870
So here's the question.

26
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:23,970
If a tumor made him do
it, is he guilty?

27
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:28,020
Most people answer that fast.

28
00:01:28,890 --> 00:01:30,060
Hold on to your answer.

29
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:34,950
By the end of this episode, we'll show
you why the same logic, followed

30
00:01:34,970 --> 00:01:38,340
all the way down, could set
almost everyone free.

31
00:01:50,290 --> 00:01:53,810
Almost everything we know about this man
comes from one paper, written

32
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:57,930
by two neurologists at the University
of Virginia, Jeffrey Burns and

33
00:01:57,950 --> 00:02:01,120
Russell Swerdlow. It was
published in 2003.

34
00:02:01,790 --> 00:02:05,710
It's four pages long, and it's more
careful than the retellings, so

35
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:07,180
we're going to stay close to it.

36
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:13,240
In 2000, he began collecting
pornography and visiting massage parlors

37
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:19,380
for sex. He hid all of it, and the
paper records why: he felt it was

38
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,710
unacceptable. Remember that.

39
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:23,330
It matters later.

40
00:02:25,070 --> 00:02:28,570
When his stepdaughter told her mother,
he was removed from the home

41
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:33,510
and found guilty. A judge gave him a
choice: an inpatient treatment

42
00:02:33,530 --> 00:02:39,090
program, or jail. He chose treatment,
and then got himself expelled,

43
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:43,300
because he couldn't stop propositioning
the staff and the other patients.

44
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:49,090
Which brings us back to that hospital,
the night before sentencing.

45
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:53,620
Over the next day, the doctors noticed
things that didn't fit a man

46
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:55,190
who was simply depraved.

47
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:56,510
His balance was off.

48
00:02:57,040 --> 00:02:59,190
He propositioned the
women examining him.

49
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:01,810
He had wet himself and
didn't seem to care.

50
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:05,650
Asked to copy a clock face,
he couldn't do it.

51
00:03:07,050 --> 00:03:08,880
Those are signs of something physical.

52
00:03:09,670 --> 00:03:14,270
The scan found a tumor, a
hemangiopericytoma, growing up from the

53
00:03:14,290 --> 00:03:17,090
base of his skull and pushing into the
right orbitofrontal cortex.

54
00:03:17,110 --> 00:03:21,470
That's a region just above the eyes
that helps weigh what we want

55
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:23,460
against what it will cost us.

56
00:03:23,910 --> 00:03:27,820
It's part of the brain's system for
saying: not now, not this.

57
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:30,910
It was removed in December 2000.

58
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:34,850
Within days, his walking and
bladder control improved.

59
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:37,730
He completed a twelve-step program.

60
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:42,140
Months later, he was judged no threat
to his stepdaughter, and he

61
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:42,710
went home.

62
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:48,830
In October 2001, he developed a
persistent headache, and he began

63
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:51,200
secretly collecting pornography again.

64
00:03:52,170 --> 00:03:54,320
A new scan showed the
tumor had come back.

65
00:03:54,790 --> 00:03:59,450
It was removed again in February 2002,
and, according to his doctors'

66
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:03,340
accounts to the press, the urges
went away a second time.

67
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:05,430
Look at the pattern.

68
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:08,470
Tumor present, behavior present.

69
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:11,270
Tumor gone, behavior gone.

70
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:13,740
Tumor back, behavior back.

71
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:18,080
On a single human being, that's about
as close to an experiment as

72
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:23,340
medicine gets. Swerdlow's own summary
was blunt: what this man needed

73
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:25,930
was an operation, not incarceration.

74
00:04:28,550 --> 00:04:32,140
That's the first piece of evidence on
the scale, and it's heavy.

75
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:37,480
But the paper also says something the
popular version usually leaves

76
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:41,740
out. The authors wrote that the tumor
likely made worse a preexisting

77
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:42,960
interest in pornography.

78
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:46,160
It didn't create his
desires out of nothing.

79
00:04:46,180 --> 00:04:51,820
And a 2023 review of sixty-four similar
cases found that they mostly

80
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:55,650
reflect a general loss of
inhibition, not a new attraction.

81
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:58,210
The tumor didn't write a new script.

82
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:00,370
It may have fired the editor.

83
00:05:01,810 --> 00:05:05,360
And one fact doesn't change,
whatever we decide about him.

84
00:05:06,070 --> 00:05:07,100
A child was harmed.

85
00:05:07,910 --> 00:05:11,020
Everything else in this
question sits on top of that.

86
00:05:17,830 --> 00:05:21,990
So, is he guilty? In most American
courts, the answer starts with

87
00:05:22,010 --> 00:05:24,420
a rule written in England in 1843.

88
00:05:25,030 --> 00:05:28,770
A man named Daniel M'Naghten shot Edward
Drummond, the private secretary

89
00:05:28,790 --> 00:05:30,920
of the Prime Minister, Robert Peel.

90
00:05:31,750 --> 00:05:33,500
Drummond died days later.

91
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:37,420
M'Naghten is generally believed to have
mistaken him for Peel himself.

92
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:42,070
He was acquitted as insane, Queen
Victoria was furious, and the judges

93
00:05:42,090 --> 00:05:44,880
were called before the House of
Lords to explain themselves.

94
00:05:45,710 --> 00:05:49,180
What they wrote still
decides cases today.

95
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:55,240
A defendant is excused if, because of a
disease of the mind, he didn't

96
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:58,390
know what he was doing, or didn't
know that it was wrong.

97
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:01,570
That's the test. It's about knowledge.

98
00:06:02,750 --> 00:06:07,260
And our man knew. The paper says his
moral knowledge was intact.

99
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:10,900
He hid what he did because he
knew it was unacceptable.

100
00:06:12,110 --> 00:06:16,870
Swerdlow put it plainly: he knew what
he was doing was wrong, and

101
00:06:16,890 --> 00:06:20,780
he didn't stop. Under the
M'Naghten rule, he's sane.

102
00:06:21,590 --> 00:06:22,280
He's guilty.

103
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:25,910
But there's another kind of test.

104
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:31,390
In 1962, the American Law Institute
proposed one with two questions.

105
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:35,090
Could the defendant appreciate
that his conduct was wrong?

106
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:37,910
Or could he conform his
conduct to the law?

107
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:42,250
That second question is the only
door that fits this man.

108
00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:44,270
His problem was never knowing.

109
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:45,510
It was stopping.

110
00:06:46,210 --> 00:06:49,760
For a while, that door was open
in most federal courts.

111
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:55,030
Then John Hinckley shot President
Reagan, and in 1982 a jury found

112
00:06:55,050 --> 00:06:57,020
him not guilty by reason of insanity.

113
00:06:57,690 --> 00:06:58,900
The backlash was immediate.

114
00:06:59,570 --> 00:07:03,240
Two years later, Congress passed the
Insanity Defense Reform Act,

115
00:07:03,430 --> 00:07:06,220
and the question of control was
cut out of federal law.

116
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:09,980
Today, the answer depends on
where you're standing.

117
00:07:09,1000 --> 00:07:15,880
As of 2020, by Justice Stephen
Breyer's count, sixteen states and

118
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:19,910
Washington, D.C. still ask whether a
defendant could control himself.

119
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:22,170
Federal courts don't.

120
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:27,160
And four states, Kansas, Idaho, Montana
and Utah, have abolished the

121
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:29,330
traditional insanity defense entirely.

122
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:33,010
In 2020, the Supreme Court
said that's allowed.

123
00:07:34,510 --> 00:07:35,200
And here's a twist.

124
00:07:36,050 --> 00:07:38,920
News reports describe our
patient as a Virginia man.

125
00:07:39,490 --> 00:07:43,030
Virginia is one of only three states
that pair the M'Naghten rule

126
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:45,080
with a test for irresistible impulse.

127
00:07:45,790 --> 00:07:49,490
If his case had ever gone to an
insanity trial, that argument was

128
00:07:49,510 --> 00:07:50,380
at least available.

129
00:07:51,130 --> 00:07:54,140
Whether a jury would have
accepted it, nobody can say.

130
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:58,390
It's also worth knowing how
rare all of this is.

131
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:02,960
In a landmark study of eight states,
the insanity defense was raised

132
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:07,240
in under one percent of felony cases,
and it succeeded in about a

133
00:08:07,260 --> 00:08:11,360
quarter of those. The idea of
criminals walking free on an insanity

134
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:12,910
plea is mostly a movie.

135
00:08:14,250 --> 00:08:18,150
So the law's answer is: it depends on
the state, and on whether you

136
00:08:18,170 --> 00:08:20,460
think his problem was
knowing or stopping.

137
00:08:21,050 --> 00:08:22,280
That isn't satisfying.

138
00:08:23,090 --> 00:08:25,200
So let's test the idea on
a harder case.

139
00:08:31,328 --> 00:08:35,988
On the evening of July 31st, 1966, a
twenty-five-year-old engineering

140
00:08:36,008 --> 00:08:40,678
student at the University of Texas sat
down at a typewriter in Austin.

141
00:08:42,538 --> 00:08:45,918
"I don't really understand myself
these days," he wrote.

142
00:08:45,938 --> 00:08:48,958
"I am supposed to be an average,
reasonable and intelligent young

143
00:08:48,978 --> 00:08:54,318
man. However, lately, I have been a
victim of many unusual and irrational

144
00:08:54,338 --> 00:08:56,068
thoughts."

145
00:08:56,768 --> 00:08:58,718
And then he asked for
something unusual.

146
00:08:59,408 --> 00:09:03,368
"After my death," he wrote, "I wish that
an autopsy would be performed

147
00:09:03,388 --> 00:09:06,078
on me to see if there is any
visible physical disorder."

148
00:09:09,238 --> 00:09:12,368
Over the next hours, he killed
his mother and his wife.

149
00:09:12,918 --> 00:09:16,578
Then he carried a footlocker of weapons
to the top of the university's

150
00:09:16,598 --> 00:09:21,358
tower. By today's count, he killed
seventeen people, including an

151
00:09:21,378 --> 00:09:23,558
unborn child, and wounded thirty-one.

152
00:09:23,578 --> 00:09:28,398
Among them were Claire Wilson,
eighteen years old and eight months

153
00:09:28,418 --> 00:09:34,458
pregnant, who survived; her son, who
didn't; and her boyfriend, Thomas

154
00:09:34,478 --> 00:09:37,848
Eckman, who was killed moments later.

155
00:09:42,148 --> 00:09:47,148
The autopsy found what he'd asked about:
a tumor, about two centimeters

156
00:09:47,168 --> 00:09:50,038
across, deep in the
middle of his brain.

157
00:09:51,578 --> 00:09:55,108
That finding became one of the most
famous stories in neuroscience.

158
00:09:55,898 --> 00:10:01,138
You may know it: a tumor pressing on
his amygdala, the brain's threat

159
00:10:01,158 --> 00:10:04,828
center, turning a quiet young
man into a killer.

160
00:10:05,578 --> 00:10:08,848
It's been told in books,
lectures and viral videos.

161
00:10:10,308 --> 00:10:12,898
Here's what the original
documents actually say.

162
00:10:13,528 --> 00:10:17,708
The pathologist who did the autopsy
called it an astrocytoma, and

163
00:10:17,728 --> 00:10:22,428
wrote: "No correlation to psychosis." A
panel convened by the Governor

164
00:10:22,448 --> 00:10:26,948
of Texas re-examined the tissue,
called it a glioblastoma, a highly

165
00:10:26,968 --> 00:10:31,338
malignant cancer, and listed five
reasons its examination was limited.

166
00:10:31,968 --> 00:10:33,898
The body had already been embalmed.

167
00:10:34,288 --> 00:10:36,358
Parts of the brain were
damaged or missing.

168
00:10:38,278 --> 00:10:39,568
Their conclusion was careful.

169
00:10:40,238 --> 00:10:44,458
The tumor, they wrote, "conceivably
could have contributed" to his

170
00:10:44,478 --> 00:10:47,008
inability to control his
emotions and actions.

171
00:10:47,618 --> 00:10:51,018
But they also wrote that existing
knowledge of the brain "does not

172
00:10:51,038 --> 00:10:52,988
enable us to explain" what he did.

173
00:10:53,638 --> 00:10:55,428
Neither document
mentions the amygdala.

174
00:10:56,198 --> 00:10:57,768
That part of the story came later.

175
00:10:59,808 --> 00:11:01,658
Now look at the other
side of the scale.

176
00:11:02,288 --> 00:11:06,568
Four months earlier, he told a university
psychiatrist he'd been thinking

177
00:11:06,588 --> 00:11:09,998
about going up on the tower with a
deer rifle and shooting people.

178
00:11:10,368 --> 00:11:12,758
He'd been up the tower
twice that year.

179
00:11:13,408 --> 00:11:17,568
On the last morning, he bought guns
at three different stores and

180
00:11:17,588 --> 00:11:19,798
packed food, water and supplies.

181
00:11:20,808 --> 00:11:25,058
He wrote down, hours ahead, that he
had decided to kill his wife.

182
00:11:25,708 --> 00:11:29,158
He'd admitted to the psychiatrist
that he had hit her before.

183
00:11:29,888 --> 00:11:32,938
And he described a father who
beat his mother for years.

184
00:11:34,538 --> 00:11:36,438
Put the two cases side by side.

185
00:11:36,458 --> 00:11:41,598
In Virginia, the tumor came with obvious
neurological signs, the behavior

186
00:11:41,618 --> 00:11:45,448
tracked the tumor, and it went away
twice when the tumor did.

187
00:11:46,138 --> 00:11:50,728
In Austin, the people who knew him
described no neurological symptoms,

188
00:11:51,338 --> 00:11:56,018
there's no before and after, and the
planning looks like a mind that

189
00:11:56,038 --> 00:11:59,288
was working, not one that
had lost control.

190
00:12:01,048 --> 00:12:03,478
So why does the tumor story survive?

191
00:12:04,488 --> 00:12:06,618
Partly because it's a better story.

192
00:12:07,408 --> 00:12:11,378
But partly, we think, because
it's a comforting one.

193
00:12:12,088 --> 00:12:15,878
If a tumor did it, then a
person like us didn't.

194
00:12:16,888 --> 00:12:18,818
Whitman himself seemed to
want that answer.

195
00:12:19,428 --> 00:12:20,438
He asked for the autopsy.

196
00:12:21,248 --> 00:12:23,598
The evidence just doesn't
give it to him.

197
00:12:29,721 --> 00:12:30,851
Here's where it gets harder.

198
00:12:31,661 --> 00:12:36,161
In 2018, a team led by the neurologist
Ryan Darby gathered seventeen

199
00:12:36,181 --> 00:12:40,211
cases where a brain lesion appeared
and criminal behavior followed.

200
00:12:40,741 --> 00:12:42,351
The lesions were in different places.

201
00:12:42,921 --> 00:12:46,321
But when the team mapped how those places
were wired, every one connected

202
00:12:46,341 --> 00:12:50,281
to the same network, regions involved in
moral judgment and in weighing

203
00:12:50,301 --> 00:12:54,641
value. The authors added a warning:
most people with damage in that

204
00:12:54,661 --> 00:12:56,351
network never commit a crime.

205
00:12:57,761 --> 00:12:59,621
And it isn't only tumors.

206
00:13:00,931 --> 00:13:04,451
Researchers at the University of
California, San Francisco, reviewed

207
00:13:04,471 --> 00:13:06,721
more than two thousand
dementia patients.

208
00:13:07,351 --> 00:13:10,871
Among those with one form,
behavioral-variant frontotemporal dementia,

209
00:13:10,891 --> 00:13:15,361
more than a third developed new
criminal behavior: theft, traffic

210
00:13:15,831 --> 00:13:18,101
violations, sexual
advances, trespassing.

211
00:13:18,711 --> 00:13:23,341
Among Alzheimer's patients, it
was under eight percent.

212
00:13:23,961 --> 00:13:28,191
For about one in seven people with
that form of dementia, a crime

213
00:13:28,211 --> 00:13:30,701
was the first sign that
anything was wrong.

214
00:13:32,281 --> 00:13:35,831
The list of brain changes that can
push behavior keeps growing.

215
00:13:36,601 --> 00:13:38,591
And that leads to the
uncomfortable part.

216
00:13:39,101 --> 00:13:40,851
All behavior comes from a brain.

217
00:13:41,661 --> 00:13:44,511
A tumor is just a cause we
can see on a scan.

218
00:13:45,301 --> 00:13:47,511
What about the causes we can't see?

219
00:13:48,321 --> 00:13:49,831
A childhood like Whitman's.

220
00:13:50,441 --> 00:13:51,571
The genes you were dealt.

221
00:13:52,121 --> 00:13:55,151
The way your brain was wired before
you made a single choice.

222
00:13:56,661 --> 00:14:00,901
The neuroscientist Robert
Sapolsky argues exactly this.

223
00:14:01,591 --> 00:14:06,531
In his 2023 book, Determined, he says
that everything we do comes

224
00:14:06,551 --> 00:14:10,851
down to the luck of our biology and
our environment, and since we

225
00:14:10,871 --> 00:14:14,591
control neither, nobody deserves
blame in the way we usually mean

226
00:14:14,611 --> 00:14:19,871
it. If the tumor excuses the man in
Virginia, his question is: why

227
00:14:19,891 --> 00:14:22,161
doesn't everything else
excuse everyone?

228
00:14:23,601 --> 00:14:27,671
The strongest answer comes from a
law professor, Stephen Morse.

229
00:14:28,201 --> 00:14:30,671
"Brains do not commit
crimes," he writes.

230
00:14:31,181 --> 00:14:35,781
"People commit crimes." In his view, a
cause can never be the excuse,

231
00:14:35,801 --> 00:14:38,091
because all behavior is caused.

232
00:14:39,001 --> 00:14:42,761
If it were, we'd have to excuse
everyone, and nobody really believes

233
00:14:42,781 --> 00:14:47,791
that. What excuses someone is losing the
capacity to respond to reasons.

234
00:14:49,511 --> 00:14:53,771
And there's a third view, from the
researchers Joshua Greene and Jonathan

235
00:14:53,791 --> 00:14:58,161
Cohen. The law, they argue, can absorb
whatever neuroscience discovers.

236
00:14:58,791 --> 00:15:02,731
But the science will slowly change our
intuitions, and push us away

237
00:15:02,751 --> 00:15:06,161
from punishment as payback, toward
punishment as prevention.

238
00:15:08,121 --> 00:15:10,391
That's the debate. Now it's our turn.

239
00:15:16,659 --> 00:15:19,669
First, the strongest case
that he's guilty.

240
00:15:20,719 --> 00:15:24,919
He knew it was wrong, and knowledge is
what the law has always asked

241
00:15:24,939 --> 00:15:29,519
about. He hid what he did, which
takes planning and self-control.

242
00:15:29,539 --> 00:15:35,299
And by his own doctors' account, the
tumor likely amplified an interest

243
00:15:35,319 --> 00:15:36,789
that was already in him.

244
00:15:37,359 --> 00:15:39,969
Plenty of people carry urges
they never act on.

245
00:15:40,439 --> 00:15:44,249
A tumor that weakens your brakes is a
lot like a bottle of whiskey,

246
00:15:44,999 --> 00:15:46,749
and we don't excuse drunk drivers.

247
00:15:48,249 --> 00:15:50,599
Now the strongest case that he isn't.

248
00:15:51,089 --> 00:15:53,159
A drunk driver chooses the bottle.

249
00:15:53,749 --> 00:15:54,859
He didn't choose the tumor.

250
00:15:55,409 --> 00:15:59,869
The change came suddenly, in his
forties, along with physical signs

251
00:15:59,889 --> 00:16:04,489
nobody could fake, like losing
control of his bladder.

252
00:16:04,509 --> 00:16:09,089
And it switched off and on
with the tumor, twice.

253
00:16:09,109 --> 00:16:13,389
If self-control means anything, he
lost it, and he got it back from

254
00:16:13,409 --> 00:16:13,959
a surgeon.

255
00:16:15,639 --> 00:16:16,469
Here's our verdict.

256
00:16:17,299 --> 00:16:21,379
He is responsible for what he did, but
far less responsible than a

257
00:16:21,399 --> 00:16:23,789
man who did the same thing
with a healthy brain.

258
00:16:24,639 --> 00:16:28,429
The right response was the one that
eventually happened: surgery,

259
00:16:28,739 --> 00:16:30,229
treatment and supervision.

260
00:16:31,059 --> 00:16:34,139
Prison alone would have punished him
and left the tumor in charge.

261
00:16:34,159 --> 00:16:37,709
Our confidence is seventy percent.

262
00:16:38,179 --> 00:16:42,019
What would change our mind: evidence
that he had acted on a sexual

263
00:16:42,039 --> 00:16:44,549
interest in children before
the tumor appeared.

264
00:16:45,299 --> 00:16:50,069
Then the tumor looks less like a cause,
and more like an accelerant.

265
00:16:53,669 --> 00:16:55,479
And the man in the tower?

266
00:16:56,189 --> 00:16:58,809
The tumor deserves a
footnote, not an acquittal.

267
00:16:58,829 --> 00:17:02,289
The record shows planning,
not a loss of control.

268
00:17:02,309 --> 00:17:04,999
Our confidence there is
eighty-five percent.

269
00:17:08,079 --> 00:17:09,969
Now the bigger question.

270
00:17:10,579 --> 00:17:13,269
Does neuroscience
eventually excuse everyone?

271
00:17:13,879 --> 00:17:17,089
Our verdict is no. A cause
is not an excuse.

272
00:17:17,699 --> 00:17:21,419
The line can't be between behavior
that's caused and behavior that

273
00:17:21,439 --> 00:17:23,689
isn't, because all of it is caused.

274
00:17:24,619 --> 00:17:28,659
The line is between a person who can
still be moved by reasons, by

275
00:17:28,679 --> 00:17:33,659
consequences, by knowing that
something is wrong, and a person who

276
00:17:33,679 --> 00:17:37,959
can't. Most people who do harm are
still on the reachable side of

277
00:17:37,979 --> 00:17:41,969
that line. The man in Virginia,
for a while, was not.

278
00:17:43,649 --> 00:17:47,949
Our confidence here is lower, sixty
percent, because this is where

279
00:17:47,969 --> 00:17:49,599
the science is moving fastest.

280
00:17:50,589 --> 00:17:54,309
What would change our mind: research
showing that the self-control

281
00:17:54,329 --> 00:17:59,269
of ordinary offenders is impaired almost
as badly as his, a difference

282
00:17:59,289 --> 00:18:01,259
of degree, not of kind.

283
00:18:02,069 --> 00:18:07,289
If that's true, the line we just drew
starts to fade, Greene and Cohen

284
00:18:07,309 --> 00:18:10,719
are right, and the purpose of
punishment has to change.

285
00:18:14,699 --> 00:18:15,509
That's our verdict.

286
00:18:16,279 --> 00:18:19,629
It's open, which means it can change.

287
00:18:20,499 --> 00:18:23,709
If you think we got it wrong, make
your case in the comments.

288
00:18:24,259 --> 00:18:26,849
We'll answer the strongest one
in the next episode.

289
00:18:30,259 --> 00:18:34,919
Next case: a car that was driving
itself, a backup driver who wasn't

290
00:18:34,939 --> 00:18:38,989
watching the road, and a woman
crossing a street in Tempe, Arizona.

291
00:18:40,019 --> 00:18:42,249
When the machine kills, who's guilty?
